WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.560
Thanks to KiwiCo for sponsoring this video.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.760
Hi I'm Toby and welcome to this video&nbsp;
where i'm going to take a little look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:14.160
at the famous story Alice in Wonderland written&nbsp;
by Lewis Carroll. It's a very creative story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:20.960
it's a bit nonsensical and at times bizarre but&nbsp;
behind some of this writing there supposedly hides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:27.840
a little bit of mathematics too. You see, Lewis&nbsp;
Carroll is actually the pen name for 19th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:35.200
mathematician Charles Dodgson and he initially&nbsp;
dreamed up the story for 10 year old Alice Liddell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:41.120
the daughter of his university college dean and&nbsp;
he told the story to her and her sisters on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:48.320
boat trip in 1864. The fact that Carroll is much&nbsp;
more famous as a writer than as a mathematician&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:55.680
perhaps speaks to the fact that his mathematical&nbsp;
work was comparatively unnoteworthy. He did write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.200
several books on mathematics including&nbsp;
an elementary treatise on determinants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.800
His work was in the fields of geometry,&nbsp;
linear and matrix algebra and mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.240
logic so let's take a look at some of the&nbsp;
math that snuck its way into the story.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.440
After falling down the rabbit hole&nbsp;
Alice is in a bit of distress. She says

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:24.000
"I'll try if I know all the things I used&nbsp;
to know. Let me see: 4 times 5 is 12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.680
and 4 times 6 is 13, and 4 times 7 is -oh&nbsp;
dear! I shall never get to 20 at that rate!"

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.280
According to Martin Gardner who has written&nbsp;
an annotated version of Alice in Wonderland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.560
the most simple explanation here is that&nbsp;
Alice is adding one every time she does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:48.240
a multiplication by four and if you keep&nbsp;
going this way you'll get to four times 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:55.120
being equal to 19 and children especially&nbsp;
only know multiplication up to the 12s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.200
so she won't be able to go any further&nbsp;
and won't be able to get to 20.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:06.000
This is a nonsense form of multiplication&nbsp;
but it sets us up for the nonsense to come.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.800
Later in the story we meet the Cheshire Cat

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:14.720
"'All right', said the Cat; and this time&nbsp;
it vanished quite slowly, beginning with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.640
end of the tail, and ending with the grin, which&nbsp;
remained some time after the rest of it had gone.&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.960
'Well I've often seen a cat without a grin,'&nbsp;
thought Alice 'but a grin without a cat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.040
it's the most curious thing&nbsp;
I ever saw in all my life.'"

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:38.640
This grin might be referring to abstract or pure&nbsp;
mathematics. That it doesn't necessarily have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.840
a cat to go along with it which would&nbsp;
be the real life applications or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:50.080
obvious uses of what the research mathematicians&nbsp;
are working on, they're doing it just for the joy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.920
of the mathematics itself and are living&nbsp;
in the abstract world of the equations.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.520
Further into wonderland&nbsp;
Alice is talking to Gryphon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.120
and Mock Turtle, she asked them about lessons

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.480
"'And how many hours a day did you do lessons?'&nbsp;
said Alice, in a hurry to change the subject.&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.240
'Ten hours the first day' said the&nbsp;
Mock Turtle: 'nine the next and so on.'&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.400
'What a curious plan!' exclaimed Alice.
'That's the reason they're called lessons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:26.080
because they lesson from day to day.'
This was quite a new idea to Alice and she thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.840
it over a little before she made her next remark.
'Then the 11th day must have been a holiday.'&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.280
'Of course it was' said the mock turtle
'and how did you manage on the 12th?'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.040
Alice went on eagerly.
'That's enough about lessons.'"

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.280
This might be a fun way to introduce&nbsp;
the idea of negative numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.760
something that also puzzled early mathematicians.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.600
In this case what would happen on the 12th day,&nbsp;
would the students start to teach the teacher?

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.800
Then we go to the famous tea party&nbsp;
scene, there's the Mad Hatter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:04.320
a hare and a mouse and Alice is also&nbsp;
informed of a fourth character, time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:10.240
who is absent from the party due to a falling out&nbsp;
with the hatter. As a result the three at the tea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:16.400
party are forever stuck at tea time they're&nbsp;
forever going around and around the table.&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:23.840
"The hare takes out a watch and Alice remarks&nbsp;
'what a funny watch! It tells the day of the month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.960
and doesn't tell what o'clock it is'
"Why should it?' muttered the hatter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:34.480
'Does your watch tell you what year it is?'
'Of course not,' Alice replied very readily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.320
'but that's because it stays the&nbsp;
same year for such a long time.'&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.160
'Which is just the case&nbsp;
with mine' said the hatter."

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.120
Later he says that "'It's always six o'clock'&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:52.080
'Is that the reason so many tea things are put&nbsp;
out here?' Alice asked 'Yes that's it' said the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.920
hatter 'it's always tea time and we've no&nbsp;
time to wash the things between whiles.'&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:04.320
'Then you keep moving around I suppose' said Alice
'Exactly so' said the hatter."

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.080
Now it might be nothing but the idea of three&nbsp;
characters stuck going around and around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:17.120
and not making much sense without time shares a&nbsp;
few similarities with the idea of quaternions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.320
something that was introduced&nbsp;
not too long before the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:26.880
was written. Quaternions consist of&nbsp;
three spatial dimensions i, j and k&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:34.560
which are imaginary and a fourth dimension which&nbsp;
is real and that is time. Perhaps at the tea party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:40.720
the three characters are the imaginary units&nbsp;
and without time they don't really make sense.

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:47.120
A property of quaternions is that when&nbsp;
you multiply them they are non-commutative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:54.640
which means the order actually matters. Normal&nbsp;
multiplication such as 3 times 4 is the same as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:06:02.080
4 times 3 it is commutative but with quaternions&nbsp;
i times j is not the same as j times i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.840
and there's a scene at the tea party&nbsp;
where this comes up in the language.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.360
They are discussing a riddle "'Do you mean that&nbsp;
you think you can find out the answer to it?'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.640
said the March Hare.
'Exactly so,' said Alice.&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.800
'Then you should say what you&nbsp;
mean' the March Hare went on.&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.880
'I do' Alice hastily replied 'at&nbsp;
least - at least I mean what I say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:26.880
that's the same thing you know.'&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:34.560
'Not the same thing a bit' said the hatter 'you&nbsp;
might just as well say that I see what I eat is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.760
the same thing as I eat what I see.'
'You might just as well say'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:46.400
added in the March hare 'that I like what I&nbsp;
get is the same thing as I get what I like.'

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:52.560
So if the characters at the tea party are used&nbsp;
to a non-commutative form of multiplication then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:58.560
that's maybe why they're so unsatisfied with Alice&nbsp;
claiming that the order of words doesn't matter.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:04.320
Something that happens a lot to Alice during&nbsp;
the story is that she's often changing size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:10.560
and having to eat or drink something to return&nbsp;
to the right proportions. A caterpillar even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:16.960
tells Alice to keep her temper and Alice&nbsp;
interprets this to mean to not get angry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:23.600
however with our mathematical eyes in place we&nbsp;
could see that Carroll is making a play on words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:27.920
and that there is a more mathematical&nbsp;
definition of temper that would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:34.240
been more familiar to victorians at the time&nbsp;
and has to do with scale and proportions.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:41.680
We see examples of both enlargements and stretches&nbsp;
which affect Alice's body differently. We tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:47.520
to use the word enlargement even if we're making&nbsp;
something smaller we would just use a scale factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:53.200
between 0 and 1 in that case and that&nbsp;
mathematical use of the word enlargement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:58.480
might make sentences like "you will grow&nbsp;
shorter" make a little bit more sense.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:05.440
Stretches would act about a point so if Alice is&nbsp;
stretched about her middle that would make every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:10.160
point twice as far away from the middle&nbsp;
as it used to be which might explain why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:16.560
her neck grows so long and in this case&nbsp;
Alice has lost her mathematical temper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:22.160
The caterpillar might be telling Alice to keep&nbsp;
her body in proportion no matter what size she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:28.640
is. Carroll did love Euclidean geometry and&nbsp;
wrote some books based on Euclid's elements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.880
and in Euclidean geometry&nbsp;
it doesn't matter what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:40.160
magnitude of shapes are all that matters is&nbsp;
the ratio between sides or the proportions.

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:44.320
I have seen many other interpretations&nbsp;
of the Alice in Wonderland story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:50.640
people claim to have found commentary on&nbsp;
social and political issues or references&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:55.920
to victorian literature. Given the author's&nbsp;
background it does make sense for there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:02.320
be a little bit of mathematics in there too but&nbsp;
maybe the nature of the story just lends itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:09.120
to these interpretations. Regardless I hope you've&nbsp;
enjoyed taking a little look down the rabbit hole&nbsp;&nbsp;

